-- Passage rg7-comparing-texts-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('baefc163-545a-59a3-9c48-fd4a4bf380b5', 'rg7-comparing-texts-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-comparing-texts'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Number, Please$q$, $q$Text 1: First Shift (a story)

In 1912, Ada Mensah took her seat at the long switchboard on her first day as a telephone operator. In front of her was a wall of small holes called jacks, and on the desk lay rows of cords with metal plugs. Twenty other young women sat beside her, each wearing a headset.

A tiny light flickered on. Ada's heart jumped. She plugged a cord into the glowing jack and said, as she had practiced, "Number, please."

"Doctor Haddad's office," said a worried voice. "Our boy has a high fever."

Ada found the right jack and pushed in the second plug. She heard the call connect, and she smiled. The supervisor walked behind the row, watching the clock. Ada worked quickly and kept her voice calm and polite, just as the rules required.

By evening, her fingers ached. But as she walked home, Ada thought about all the voices she had joined together. The town felt smaller somehow, and friendlier.

Text 2: Telephone Operators (a factual account)

In the early 1900s, most telephone calls could not go through without help. A caller lifted the receiver, which lit a small lamp on a switchboard at the telephone office. An operator answered, asked for the number, and connected the call by plugging a cord into the correct jack.

Most operators were young women. Telephone companies set strict rules. Operators were expected to work fast, speak politely and use set phrases. Supervisors often timed how quickly calls were answered.

Over the following decades, telephone companies installed automatic systems that let callers dial numbers themselves. As these systems spread, fewer operators were needed to connect ordinary calls.$q$, 'draft')
