insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('e2e4e3e6-d5e5-51ad-886e-212ec0064da4', 'rg7-comparing-texts-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-comparing-texts'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Roads of Silk and Sand$q$, $q$Text 1: The Long Road (a story)

At fifteen, Liang Wei left the city of his birth with his uncle's camel caravan. The camels carried bolts of shining silk, wrapped in cloth against the sand.

For months they crossed deserts and mountain passes. At every oasis town, they rested at an inn built around a courtyard, where camels drank and travelers from distant lands traded stories. Liang learned a few words in four new languages. He tasted dates, saw glass as clear as water and watched a merchant write on paper with strange letters.

At last, after nearly two years, Liang stood on a harbor wall and looked out at a sea he had only heard about. His uncle sold the final bolt of silk to a merchant with a red cap. Liang realized he was carrying something home that no camel could hold: everything he had learned on the way.

Text 2: The Silk Road (a factual account)

The Silk Road was not a single road. It was a network of trade routes that linked China with Central Asia, the Middle East and the lands around the Mediterranean Sea. People used these routes for well over a thousand years.

Very few traders traveled the whole distance. Instead, goods usually passed from merchant to merchant, changing hands many times along the way. Camels carried loads across deserts, and caravans stopped at oasis towns and at roadside inns called caravanserais.

Silk was only one of many goods. Traders also exchanged spices, glass, paper, metals and much more. Just as important, the routes carried ideas. Languages, inventions, art and beliefs spread from place to place along with the goods.$q$, 'draft')
