   $q$Why might the author of Text 1 have changed the usual pattern of trade described in Text 2?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The author wanted to prove that Text 2 is wrong about how goods moved along the routes.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-fiction-fact"},{"text":"A single long journey lets one character see the many places and peoples along the routes.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The author wanted to show that silk was the only good that traders cared about at all.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"A single long journey makes the story shorter than describing many separate merchants.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A single long journey lets one character see the many places and peoples along the routes.$q$, $q$By sending Liang the whole way, the story can show deserts, inns, new languages and a distant sea through one person's eyes. Fiction often reshapes history for this reason; it does not claim the factual account is wrong.$q$, 'draft', 'e2e4e3e6-d5e5-51ad-886e-212ec0064da4')
